WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.334 --> 00:00:15.115
"Normal" life for the March girls began bright and early the next day.

2
00:00:15.116 --> 00:00:19.733
Unfortunately, neither Meg nor Jo was in the best of moods.

3
00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:27.035
Like most siblings, they did what they always do when things are annoying and irritable: they took it out on each other.

4
00:00:27.036 --> 00:00:32.091
"Hurry up, Jo," snapped Meg as she put on her thick winter coat.

5
00:00:32.092 --> 00:00:39.018
"Why? Are you so anxious to see your precious King children?" Jo retorted nastily.

6
00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:42.011
"Must you doodle at your desk every morning?

7
00:00:42.012 --> 00:00:46.267
For once can't you keep an eye on the clock?" Meg spat back.

8
00:00:46.268 --> 00:00:53.206
Jo woke up at the crack of dawn every morning and rushed up to her sanctuary where she "doodled" on her latest story.

9
00:00:53.207 --> 00:01:00.631
She called it her "pre-day exercise" and often forgot the time until one of the others had to go up and remind her.

10
00:01:00.632 --> 00:01:05.125
"Girls, do you think it's wise to start the New Year off like this?"

11
00:01:05.126 --> 00:01:08.485
Mother gave the girls a quick kiss and hug.

12
00:01:08.486 --> 00:01:17.726
As she did every cold winter day, she had baked two buns for them, not for eating but to keep their hands warm from the frigid temperature.

13
00:01:17.727 --> 00:01:22.012
She slipped one inside each of the girls' coat pockets.

14
00:01:22.013 --> 00:01:27.054
Immediately the girls felt a surge of warmth spread across their stomachs.

15
00:01:27.055 --> 00:01:33.271
Perhaps it was that or Mother's gentle smile that made Meg and Jo stop arguing.

16
00:01:33.272 --> 00:01:36.395
"We'll try harder, Mother," promised Meg.

17
00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:42.401
"It's my fault and I apologize, Meg," admitted Jo.

18
00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:46.357
The sisters started out on the path toward the street.

19
00:01:46.358 --> 00:01:54.810
As part of their routine, they looked back before turning the corner, for Mother was always at the window, smiling and waving at them.

20
00:01:54.811 --> 00:02:02.595
No matter what happened or whatever mood they were in, that small gesture filled their hearts with love and hope.

21
00:02:02.596 --> 00:02:07.568
Today Jo caught a glimpse of someone else looking out a window next door.

22
00:02:07.569 --> 00:02:13.600
It was Laurie, looking pathetically tiny in comparison with the massive house he lived in.

23
00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:22.548
"There's Laurie!" Jo cried excitedly. She held up her foot and wiggled it at him. He promptly returned the greeting.

24
00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:28.537
Then to Jo's astonishment, Meg also lifted her foot and waved it.

25
00:02:28.538 --> 00:02:31.892
"Meg!" cried her astonished sister.

26
00:02:31.893 --> 00:02:38.086
"I'm only showing him that my ankle is better now, thanks to him," was Meg's explanation.

27
00:02:38.087 --> 00:02:44.018
Giggling and linking their arms to stay warm, the sisters hurried along the street.

28
00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:52.679
After a particularly trying day at Aunt March's, Jo dragged her feet home, eager to curl up on the sofa and read her book.

29
00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:59.443
As she approached the familiar grounds, something made her lift her head up in the direction of the Laurences' mansion.

30
00:02:59.444 --> 00:03:06.778
In the same window he had appeared in that morning, there stood Laurie, wearing what seemed to be the same attire as well.

31
00:03:06.779 --> 00:03:11.904
"That lazy boy hasn't changed all day," Jo remarked.

32
00:03:11.905 --> 00:03:18.212
"Hello, Jo!" Laurie opened his window and shouted down at her in a hoarse voice.

33
00:03:18.213 --> 00:03:21.385
"Are you sick?" Jo yelled back.

34
00:03:21.386 --> 00:03:26.755
The sudden cold air from the open window set Laurie off in a coughing fit.

35
00:03:26.756 --> 00:03:31.115
"Don't bother answering that question," Jo replied firmly.

36
00:03:31.116 --> 00:03:35.288
"I can see you're not well. Just stay put. I'm coming over!"

37
00:03:35.289 --> 00:03:44.014
If anything, Jo was loyal to her friends. If they were sick in bed, she would make any sacrifices necessary to cheer them up.

38
00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:50.748
Forgetting her own fatigue, Jo sprinted inside her house to change and gather a few things for her sick neighbor.

39
00:03:50.749 --> 00:03:54.201
Of course Jo had to get Mother's permission first.

40
00:03:54.202 --> 00:04:00.347
When Mother heard how sick Laurie was, she got Jo to take over some homemade soup.

41
00:04:00.348 --> 00:04:10.790
Jo did not often lack words to express anything but when she stepped into the Laurence mansion a few minutes later, she was speechless.

42
00:04:10.791 --> 00:04:18.442
Dark, exquisite furniture decorated the foyer, each piece intricately carved in exotic Asian wood.

43
00:04:18.443 --> 00:04:23.252
A massive crystal chandelier hung stately in the center of the hall.

44
00:04:23.253 --> 00:04:27.321
Large oil paintings of the Laurence men hung on the walls.

45
00:04:27.322 --> 00:04:32.240
There was one of a younger man who had the same mischievous grin as Laurie.

46
00:04:32.241 --> 00:04:36.480
Jo concluded that he must be Laurie's deceased father.

47
00:04:36.481 --> 00:04:41.472
She recognized the painting right next to that as old Mr. Laurence.

48
00:04:41.473 --> 00:04:46.294
"Does he frighten you?" a scratchy voice called from the top of the stairs.

49
00:04:46.295 --> 00:04:52.953
Jo looked up to see Laurie, in a maroon silk dressing gown, gazing down at her with a teasing grin.

50
00:04:52.954 --> 00:04:58.923
"I don't get frightened of anything or anyone," Jo stated overconfidently.

51
00:04:58.924 --> 00:05:07.556
Inside, however, she admitted to herself that if she came across Mr. Laurence face-to-face, she might feel a little bit intimidated.

52
00:05:07.557 --> 00:05:12.556
"Don't just stand there staring," Laurie's cheerful voice broke into her thoughts.

53
00:05:12.557 --> 00:05:15.451
"What did you bring for the sick boy?"

54
00:05:15.452 --> 00:05:19.103
Jo had almost forgotten about the basket of goodies.

55
00:05:19.104 --> 00:05:21.418
"Mother made a fresh pot of soup.

56
00:05:21.419 --> 00:05:25.334
When I told her about you, she sent it over immediately."

57
00:05:25.335 --> 00:05:30.168
Lowering his eyes, Laurie said quietly, "I wish she was my mother.

58
00:05:30.169 --> 00:05:33.765
In fact, I have a secret to tell you."

59
00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:37.852
Jo, who loved secrets, urged him to tell her.

60
00:05:37.853 --> 00:05:42.461
"But you have to promise you won't laugh," he said solemnly.

61
00:05:42.462 --> 00:05:51.720
"Okay," Jo said with uncertainty, for she found it very hard not to laugh when she thought something was truly funny.

62
00:05:51.721 --> 00:05:55.361
Laurie came down the stairs and stood next to Jo.

63
00:05:55.362 --> 00:05:59.492
Jo did not realize how tall he was until that moment.

64
00:05:59.493 --> 00:06:03.016
"I have often watched you and your sister play.

65
00:06:03.017 --> 00:06:08.492
From the window in my room, I can look into your living room," he said shyly.

66
00:06:08.493 --> 00:06:13.665
"Oh, you naughty boy! You were spying on us!" Jo exclaimed.

67
00:06:13.666 --> 00:06:18.400
"No, no!" protested Laurie. "I wasn't spying.

68
00:06:18.401 --> 00:06:26.885
I was merely curious and," he paused, "a little jealous at how happy your family always seems to be."

69
00:06:26.886 --> 00:06:32.761
Jo and her family knew of Laurie's misfortunes, though not in detail.

70
00:06:32.762 --> 00:06:37.313
His parents had died in a tragic accident when he was a small boy.

71
00:06:37.314 --> 00:06:43.856
Having no other relations except for his grandfather, Laurie had been sent to boarding schools abroad.

72
00:06:43.857 --> 00:06:48.568
Then last year, his grandfather had brought Laurie over to live with him.

73
00:06:48.569 --> 00:06:52.699
"I've always wanted a big family," he said wistfully.

74
00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:59.938
Something tugged at Jo's heart and she longed to give him a big hug just as she would have with her sisters.

75
00:06:59.939 --> 00:07:05.915
"Well, you have my permission to watch us whenever you like," she replied.

76
00:07:05.916 --> 00:07:12.128
"In fact, I need to ask Mother first, but I'm sure she will be more than happy to have you visit."

77
00:07:12.129 --> 00:07:16.845
"Thank you, Jo," he said sincerely. "I would really like that.

78
00:07:16.846 --> 00:07:22.669
Now, perhaps I should test that soup out to see whether it has gotten cold or not."

79
00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:27.459
So the two friends walked to the kitchen, laughing all the way.

80
00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:35.738
Laurie and Jo chatted away like two old friends with so much in common. Jo told him all about her sisters.

81
00:07:35.739 --> 00:07:44.818
"Well, now I'm happy to know who Beth is and who Amy is," said Laurie, who had been confused about the younger girls' names.

82
00:07:44.819 --> 00:07:51.806
"By the way, Beth sent this over to you," Jo said, taking out an old rag doll that Beth treasured.

83
00:07:51.807 --> 00:07:59.242
"Whenever she feels sick, Lucy," Jo pointed, indicating the doll's name, "makes her feel better."

84
00:07:59.243 --> 00:08:06.783
Slapping his knee, Laurie nearly tumbled off his chair, but stopped suddenly when he caught sight of someone standing in the doorway.

85
00:08:06.784 --> 00:08:18.644
"Why aren't you in bed resting, Theodore, instead of acting like a silly monkey?" the old man said in a dangerously low voice.

86
00:08:18.645 --> 00:08:25.100
Old Mr. Laurence was home and he was not happy.
